After rename feature branch in your local and when you are trying to push to GitHub remote and
rename branch is not reflecting in the GitHub remote the use the below command and check
GitHub remote.

1.git push -u origin feature/branch_new1
-u is just the short form of --set-upstream.
2.delete the old branch GitHub remote
git push origin --delete feature/branch
==============================================================
How to Rename a Git Branch (Local & Remote) — The Right Way
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ZZQKMPLnWaM

Q.This below command is mandatory?
git push --set-upstream origin feature/branch_new1
A.No, the exact command is not mandatory. But after renaming a local branch, you need to push
the new branch to the remote and set its upstream if you want normal
git push / git pull behavior.

Recommended
git push --set-upstream origin feature/branch_new1
This does two things:
1.Creates feature/branch_new1 in the Git remote.
2.Connects your local branch to that remote branch.

After that, you can simply do:
git push
git pull

Without --set-upstream
You can also do:
git push origin feature/branch_new1

This pushes the branch, but it may not set the tracking/upstream relationship. You may then
need to specify the branch again for future pushes.

For your case
Since you renamed:

feature/branch
       ↓
feature/branch_new1

I recommend:
git push -u origin feature/branch_new1
-u is just the short form of --set-upstream.

Then your relationship becomes:

Local                              Remote
feature/branch_new1  ──────────→  origin/feature/branch_new1

After verifying the new branch is correct, you can delete the old remote branch if it's no longer
needed:
git push origin --delete feature/branch
So, mandatory? No. Recommended in your situation? Yes.

Q.once we rename the feature branch in local it is possible to automatically changes the same
branch in Git Repo?
A.Yes, it is possible, but it depends on how you rename it.

If you rename only the local branch
For example:
Old local:  feature/branch
New local:  feature/branch_new1

GitHub will not automatically rename the remote branch just because you renamed your local branch.
You will have:
Local:
feature/branch_new1

Remote:
origin/feature/branch

That's what happened in your screenshot.

To rename both correctly
After renaming the local branch, push the new branch and set its upstream:
git push --set-upstream origin feature/branch_new1

Then, if the old remote branch is no longer needed:
git push origin --delete feature/branch

One exception

If you rename a branch directly on GitHub, GitHub can handle the remote branch rename and may
provide/update references for you. But renaming the branch locally in IntelliJ does not by itself
 rename the GitHub branch.

So remember:
Local rename ≠ Remote rename

You need to push the new branch (and normally delete the old remote branch) to make the remote
repository match.